s</h3><p></p><p>These are the conceptual points that often appear in theore=
tical questions.</p><ul><li><p><b>Greedy Approach:</b> K-means is a greedy =
algorithm. This means that in each iteration, it makes the choice that prov=
ides the biggest immediate improvement (lowest SSE).</p></li><li><p><b>Loca=
l Optimum:</b> Because it is greedy, K-means <b>is guaranteed to converge</=
b>, but it is <b>NOT guaranteed to find the absolute best clustering (Globa=
l Optimum)</b>. It often converges to a <i>Local Optimum</i> (a good, but n=
ot necessarily the best, solution).</p></li><li><p><b>Dependence on Initial=
ization:</b> The final clustering result can be highly dependent on the <b>=
initial random placement</b> of the <span class=3D"math-inline" data-math=
=3D"k"><span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true=
"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.6944em;"></=
span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.0315em;">k</s=
pan></span></span></span></span> centroids. (This is why some implementatio=
ns run K-means multiple times with different random starts).</p></li><li><p=
><b>Requires <span class=3D"math-inline" data-math=3D"k"><span class=3D"kat=
ex"><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><s=
pan class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class=3D"mord =
mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.0315em;">k</span></span></span></span>=
</span>:</b> You <b>must</b> specify the number of clusters (<span class=3D=
"math-inline" data-math=3D"k"><span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-ht=
ml" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=
=3D"height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"marg=
in-right: 0.0315em;">k</span></span></span></span></span>) beforehand.</p><=
/li><li><p><b>Distance Metric:</b> It typically uses <b>Euclidean distance<=
/b> (the straight-line distance, which is minimized by minimizing the squar=
ed distance).</p></li><li><p><b>Brute-Force Infeasibility:</b> It's importa=
nt to know that trying every possible partition is infeasible (Stirling num=
